@baseUrl = http://localhost:8080
@adminToken = replace-with-admin-token
# Run against a disposable local DB. Use a product and a variant of that product.
@productId = replace-with-product-id
@variantId = replace-with-variant-id

### Adjust product stock — 201; created_by is the authenticated account name, not its ID
POST {{baseUrl}}/products/{{productId}}/stock/adjust
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"quantity_change":1,"reason":"Creator name check"}

### Adjust variant stock — 201; same creator name
POST {{baseUrl}}/products/{{productId}}/stock/adjust
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"variant_id":{{variantId}},"quantity_change":1,"reason":"Creator name check"}

### Manual reduction with a name — 201; created_by is "มายด์"; stored actor remains user ID
POST {{baseUrl}}/products/{{productId}}/stock/adjust
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"quantity_change":-1,"reason":"Damaged","operator_name":"  มายด์  "}

### Blank operator uses the account name — 201
POST {{baseUrl}}/products/{{productId}}/stock/adjust
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"variant_id":{{variantId}},"quantity_change":-1,"reason":"Damaged","operator_name":"   "}

### Invalid operator type — 400; no stock changes
POST {{baseUrl}}/products/{{productId}}/stock/adjust
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{"quantity_change":-1,"reason":"Damaged","operator_name":123}

### Global history — 200; created_by resolves account IDs to names
GET {{baseUrl}}/stock/transactions
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### Product history — 200; same creator names
GET {{baseUrl}}/products/{{productId}}/stock/transactions
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### Variant history — 200; same creator names
GET {{baseUrl}}/variants/{{variantId}}/stock/transactions
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

# created_by uses the explicit adjustment name first, otherwise the account name/email.
# POS stock history uses the actual linked order's operator_name or staff name snapshot,
# including existing orders. Create a named POS order using order-operator-name.http,
# then check its stock entries in all three histories above; no second name input is needed.
# Unknown IDs and legacy labels (such as local-seed) remain unchanged; NULL stays NULL.
# The database still stores the authenticated user ID. Manual references to an order
# do not make an adjustment a POS transaction or select that order's operator name.
